# live-config "already configured" marker for its openssh-server component.
#
# live-config's 1160-openssh-server regenerates every missing SSH host key —
# and this image ships with NO host keys on purpose (9600-sysible-firstboot
# strips them so the public ISO can't be used to impersonate a Sysible box).
# So on every live boot that component ran `ssh-keygen` three times INSIDE
# live-config.service, which is Type=oneshot, DefaultDependencies=no and
# Before=basic.target: the entire boot waits on it, with the oneshot default
# TimeoutStartSec=infinity. ssh-keygen draws from getrandom(), which BLOCKS
# until the kernel CRNG is initialised, and live-config is not ordered after
# systemd-random-seed.service — nothing says the seed is credited first. On a
# machine that is slow to gather early entropy that is an unbounded wait, seen
# as "A start job is running for live-config.service (… / no limit)".
#
# The presence of this file makes live-config's component_was_executed() true,
# so 1160-openssh-server exits in Init and never runs. Key generation belongs to
# sysible-regenerate-ssh-host-keys.service instead: same `ssh-keygen -A`, but
# WantedBy=multi-user.target and Before=ssh.service — i.e. after basic.target
# and after the random seed, where blocking on entropy costs a moment rather
# than the boot, and where nothing else is waiting on it.
#
# Skipping it also stops that component undoing a deliberate choice: its last
# act is `sed -i 's|#\(PasswordAuthentication\) yes|\1 no|'`, which turns off
# the password authentication 9000-sysible-system explicitly turns ON.
